From: Pedro Baltazar <pedrobtz@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] Ignore </selectedcontent> with no selectedcontent in scope

The in-body handler for a </selectedcontent> end tag calls
implicitly_close_tags() unconditionally. With no selectedcontent element
open, that pops every element on the stack of open elements looking for
one, then dereferences the NULL current node: an assertion failure in
debug builds and a NULL dereference in release builds, from input as
small as

  <p>x</selectedcontent>

Every other end tag handled this way first checks that a matching
element is in scope, records a parse error and ignores the token when it
is not. Do the same here.

Found by fuzzing zuhtml's copy under UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer. To be
offered upstream at https://codeberg.org/gumbo-parser/gumbo-parser.
---
--- a/parser.c
+++ b/parser.c
@@ -2876,6 +2876,13 @@
     state->_selectedcontent_state = GUMBO_SELECTEDCONTENT_EMPTY;
     return true;
   } else if (tag_is(token, kEndTag, GUMBO_TAG_SELECTEDCONTENT)) {
+    // Without a selectedcontent element in scope, implicitly_close_tags()
+    // would pop every open element looking for one.
+    if (!has_an_element_in_scope(parser, GUMBO_TAG_SELECTEDCONTENT)) {
+      parser_add_parse_error(parser, token);
+      ignore_token(parser);
+      return false;
+    }
     implicitly_close_tags(parser, token, GUMBO_NAMESPACE_HTML, token->v.end_tag);
     state->_selectedcontent_target = NULL;
     return true;
